# Lesson 5, new exercise: opener has both majors, then accepts an invitation.
title: Both majors, and a maximum
id: L05-10
type: exercise
lesson: 5
concepts: 5.2, 5.6
student: S
board: 19
dealer: S
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  AQ82   QJ4    873    953
  E  76     T32    J42    KJT74
  S  JT43   AK86   AKT    Q6
  W  K95    975    Q965   A82

auction:
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: open 1NT.
  W Pass
  N 2C     = Stayman: do you have a 4-card major?
  E Pass
  S 2H*    = Four hearts (maybe four spades too)
     key: With both majors, bid hearts first.
     not 2S: With both majors, bid 2♥ first. If partner has spades, you'll still find out.
     not 2D: 2♦ says "no 4-card major." You have two.
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = 8-9 points, four spades, not four hearts
  E Pass
  S 4S*    = A maximum, with four spades
     key: Partner's major is spades, and you have 17: bid game in spades.
     not Pass: You have the top of your range and a spade fit. Accept.
     not 3NT: You've found an 8-card spade fit. Play in spades.
     not 3S: With a maximum, bid the game yourself.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You bid hearts first, as you should with both majors. North's 2NT said "not
  hearts, 8-9 points," and since Stayman promised a major, North must have
  spades. With a maximum and four spades yourself, you bid 4♠. South is
  declarer, because South bid spades first, and so West will lead to the first
  trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 5
  remind: What is the contract?

play:
  1   W D5  N D3  E D2  S DT
  2   S SJ  W S5  N S2*  E S6
      S2 key: Let the jack run. If West has the king, it can't escape.
      S2 not SQ: Did you think about the finesse? If West has the king, your jack wins.
      S2 not SA: Did you think about the finesse? If West has the king, your jack wins.
      note: Draw trumps.
  3   S ST  W S9  N S8*  E S7
      S8 key: Same again: let the ten run.
      S8 not SA: Did you notice West may still have the king? Let the ten run.
      note: Repeat the finesse: lead the ten.
  4   S S4  W SK  N SA  E H2
      note: Trumps are drawn.
  5   N C9  E C4  S C6  W CA
      note: Trumps are drawn. Lead a club: you have a loser there anyway.
  6   W H9  N H4  E H3  S HK
  7   S HA  W H5  N HJ  E HT
      note: Cash your top hearts.
      ok: D
  8   S DA  W D6  N D7  E D4
      note: Now cash your top diamonds.
      ok: H
  9   S DK  W D9  N D8  E DJ
      note: And the other one.
  10  S H8  W H7  N HQ  E C7
      note: Lead a heart over to dummy's queen.
  11  N C5  E CK  S CQ  W C2
      note: From dummy, lead a club toward your queen.
  12  E CJ  S S3  W C8  N C3
  13  S H6  W DQ  N SQ  E CT
      note: 11 tricks: 4♠ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: draw trumps by leading toward dummy's ace-queen, so the finesse
  can catch the ♠K, then cash your winners.

  4♠ made 11 tricks. 3NT would have gone down. Bidding hearts first lost
  nothing: partner showed spades next, and you found the fit.
